On that ban the options break as:
* leg = l e g : e present, third g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* dam = d a m : m present, third m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* rat = r a t : r present, first letter r not in positive set, third t ∈ {b,d,t,w}
* car = c a r : r present, third r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin = b i n : no e,r,p,m present. It is C V C, but uses i,n which never occur in the positives and third n ∉ {b,d,t,w}.

Hence four options violate the e,r,p,m ban and bin is the only e,r,p,m-free option. Colleagues agree on the ban and on bin being the unique e,r,p,m-free choice. Disagreement is interpretive: Colleague 1/2 note leg is the canonical illustration of the ban, being the clearest non-pattern example with e like ear/ego/ate, while also calling bin the textbook odd-one-out as the only option that stays clear of the e,r,p,m block. The question as phrased asks for a word that does not follow the pattern; under the ban reading A-D all do not follow, with leg being the most prototypical. Under an odd-one-out reading bin is the one that can be made to follow the pattern while the rest cannot. Unresolved is how strictly to enforce the exact positive letter inventory and third-letter {b,d,t,w} restriction versus the weaker e,r,p,m ban that is directly evidenced by the negative exemplars.

